1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,460
If you've ever checked your ad account and seen Meta spending all of your ad budget on a creative that is just not getting good performance,

2
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:14,720
and you constantly ask yourself, "What is Meta doing, and why is Meta putting spend on these, quote unquote, 'bad ads'?"

3
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:23,480
Well, in this week's video, I'm actually going to help you understand what Meta is doing, and essentially break down why Meta is spending so much money on your, again, "bad ads",

4
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:33,480
and how you can actually use this as a learning to be able to make ads that can actually scale, and not only suck up all the spend in your ad account, but also get great performance.

5
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:43,980
And if you actually stay and watch to the end of the video, I'm actually going to show you a day-by-day example of a new super winner that we launched in one of our client ad accounts that went from essentially $0 to over $3,000 a day in spend

6
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:49,880
just by testing ads the way that we do, and trusting Meta to spend on ads the right way.

7
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:52,440
So, with all that said, let's go ahead and jump in to this week's video.

8
00:00:52,580 --> 00:01:02,440
All right. So, as you see on screen, we're inside of an ad account, and for the sake of protecting our client's data, these things are going to be blurred, but I will actually tell you whether or not these ads are the same, or what is actually happening.

9
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:07,240
But essentially, as you can see here, last month we spent about $58,000 in this one campaign specifically.

10
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:14,540
And as you can see, our top-spending ad spent about $7,500 with a return on ad spend of 1.58.

11
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:26,360
Now, for a lot of people, this is where it drives you crazy, because you're looking at Meta, and you're saying, "Okay, Meta, why are we spending $7,500 on this ad to get a 1.58,

12
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:31,180
when for example, down here, we have this ad that is getting a 2.97?

13
00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:39,860
Like, Meta, what are you doing? Just spend more on this ad. Why are you spending money on all these other ads that are getting a sub-2x ROAS when we could be getting a 3?"

14
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:50,380
Now, the reason why Meta is not spending more on this ad, and the reason why it's actually spending more on this ad specifically, is because of one thing and one thing only, and that one thing is scale, okay?

15
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:56,220
You have to understand that Meta is essentially a machine that wants you to spend more on ads, okay?

16
00:01:56,220 --> 00:02:06,700
And at the end of the day, it wants you, based off of the settings that you have given it, it wants to get you the highest volume of conversions at the lowest cost possible.

17
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:12,960
And so, the key point there is volume. Like I said, Meta wants you to scale, a.k.a., it wants you to have volume.

18
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:28,120
And so, by breaking this down, you can see that essentially Meta is telling us that, "Hey, I'm going to spend the most amount of budget on this ad because I believe it will get us the highest volume of purchases at the lowest cost possible."

19
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:34,920
Now, does it have the lowest cost in the account? Absolutely not, but what it does have is it has the highest volume.

20
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:42,860
And a lot of the times—we're going to go through a few different accounts here—you will notice that the top-spending ad has the most amount of purchases.

21
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:52,620
And if you ask every single marketer out there that has scaled to even just, you know, $500,000 a month, or $1,000,000 a month, or for some of the brands that we work with, $10 million a month,

22
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:59,940
they will always take a high volume of purchases over an ad that doesn't get a lot of spend and has a good-looking ROAS, okay?

23
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:06,300
And so, I'm going to actually help you understand though a level deeper of why that's happening, but just know essentially Meta is trying to push for volume.

24
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:18,880
And so, as you can see here, you might even be looking at this ad right here, which is at a 1.97, has a $65 CPA, and as you can see, Meta only decided to spend about $3,800 on this ad.

25
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:29,940
Now, the question that you need to ask yourself before, in this instance, turning off your top-spending bad ad—because again, this top-spending ad is getting a 1.58x return on ad spend.

26
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:36,940
This ad right here has a 1.97, this ad has a 1.83, and this ad has a 2.97. So, technically, I should turn this off.

27
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:44,260
Now, that is where you're making a colossal mistake, and a lot of the times you're actually ruining your Facebook ad performance by doing that,

28
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:51,260
because what's happening here is this ad is actually carrying essentially this entire account, okay?

29
00:03:51,540 --> 00:04:00,960
And on top of that, just from a numbers perspective, this ad right here is almost at two times the amount of scale of the next best ad.

30
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:07,660
And so, the question that you need to ask yourself before actually turning off your top-spending, again, in quotations, "bad ad"—

31
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:18,780
and the reason why I keep saying in quotations is because my belief is that your top-spending ad, especially if it's an ad that had performance at some point or has been validated—I will talk about specific examples where you can have ads get a lot of spend

32
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:23,760
and not actually work at all, and so I will dive into that, but for 99% of the cases,

33
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:28,620
for most of you guys, your top-spending ad is actually probably your best ad, okay?

34
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:35,980
A lot of you guys have your mindset completely wrong, where you think the top-spending ad that has a low ROAS is a bad ad. I promise you it is not.

35
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:41,800
But let's continue to dive in. So again, talking about elements of scale here, let's say we wanted to turn off this ad.

36
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:54,580
The question, what I have everybody in Evolve ask themselves, is if I was to take this $7,500, and put this $7,500 into this ad here, or even the top two ads in regards to spend,

37
00:04:54,840 --> 00:05:02,400
Do I think that I would be able to get a better return on ad spend than the 1.58 that I'm getting? Well, let's just think through it.

38
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:07,020
Okay, well, here we're spending $3,800, which is essentially half of what we're spending here.

39
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:13,900
If I was to double the budget on this ad, do I think my return on ad spend would be better than a 1.58?

40
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:21,740
And for any of you who have experience actually scaling ad accounts, you know that, a lot of the times, whenever you increase budget, your ROAS goes down.

41
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:26,700
And now, the rate that it goes down is completely dependent on the ad creative itself and on the brand itself.

42
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:36,340
But in most instances, if you scale 20%, a lot of the times, your return on ad spend is going to drop anywhere from 5 to 25% as well.

43
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:41,280
And so, every single time you increase budget, a.k.a try to reach more people with that ad,

44
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:47,840
the overall efficiency goes down because you're forcing Meta to show your ad to more people.

45
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:53,460
So, to bring this point back home, the thing that you need to ask yourself before turning off the top-spending ad

46
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:58,840
is, okay, well, if I know that if I increase my budget, my ROAS is going to go down,

47
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:07,500
well, if I, in this instance, were to, let's say, triple this budget, do I think my return on ad spend would be better than a 1.58

48
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:12,140
if this ad right here that's getting a 1.97, and, again, half of the spend,

49
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:16,500
but because we're turning this off, we'd have to take this 7,500 and put it on this ad,

50
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:20,740
do I think if by tripling this ad spend I would have better than a 1.58?

51
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:23,100
And if you ask me, that answer's absolutely not, right?

52
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:26,360
So, in that instance, I'm not going to turn that off, right?

53
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:31,440
So, that is one of the easiest ways just to figure out whether or not you should turn off a high-spending ad,

54
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:35,840
whereas, let's take a look at this ad, for instance, which is an ad that we did actually turn off,

55
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:41,300
where you can see it was spending $2,000, and it had a 0.78x return on ad spend.

56
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:47,860
Now, we turned this ad off because we were like, okay, well, this is getting a 0.78, and this $2,000 budget

57
00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:54,360
can filter into, let's say, this ad right here that's getting a 2.97, this ad that's getting a 1.83, and this ad that's getting a 1.97,

58
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:58,180
and it's probably going to push majority of the budget up into the top-spending ad.

59
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:04,200
So, do I think I could get a better return on ad spend than 0.78 by spending an extra $2,000 on this ad?

60
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:10,580
I would say so, right? That would be essentially adding close to around 25% in budget, or whatever it may be, to this ad,

61
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:17,480
and I think if I added 25% in budget to this ad right here that's getting a 1.58, it would probably do better than a 0.78.

62
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:22,320
And so, that is the framework that we use to essentially make decisions in the ad account

63
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:27,200
is, okay, if I turn off this ad, and this spend has to go elsewhere, what is going to happen?

64
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:33,860
That's why, a lot of the times, when you guys turn off these high-spending ads, or these ads that are, again, bad ads because the ROAS is low,

65
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:39,700
meanwhile they're getting all the spend in your ad account, the rest of your account starts to do worse as a result, okay?

66
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:44,840
And so, that is the first and most important thing that you need to understand, and the framework that you need to use

67
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:50,600
is, okay, if I am to take this spend, I'm going to turn this ad off, now all of a sudden, this spend has to go into all these other ads,

68
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:57,300
do I think it will be able to uphold that additional ad spend? And most of the time, the answer is going to be no.

69
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:02,660
Now, again, in some instances, like this ad here, you can see we had at $2,000 in budget, 0.78,

70
00:08:03,020 --> 00:08:09,160
I'm looking at my top four ads up here, I'm like, hey, you know what? I feel like this $2,000 could be more efficiently spent on these other ads.

71
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:11,200
So, then, in that instance, we'll turn it off.

72
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:16,240
Now, let's go ahead and take a deeper dive, though, into, for example, the second thing that I actually mentioned,

73
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:21,520
which is around how these ads that are getting the majority of spend in your account are usually carrying the account,

74
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:24,500
just by scrolling over slightly to frequency.

75
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,440
Now, one thing that I want you to notice here is that our frequency on this top ad is 1.89, okay?

76
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:36,560
And so, what that essentially means is that people on average are seeing this 1.89 times.

77
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:41,320
So, if I am scrolling through Facebook and I see this ad, I am probably going to see it a second time.

78
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:43,260
Facebook is just going to serve it to me a second time.

79
00:08:43,380 --> 00:08:47,500
That's why you need to pay attention to reach and impressions, because what this tells you

80
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:53,780
is essentially, hey, we reached 220,000 individual people, but overall total impressions is 418,000, okay?

81
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:59,840
And so, some people may have seen this four times, some people may have seen it once, but on average, the frequency is 1.89.

82
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:06,960
And now, the reason why this metric matters is because it's actually going to tell you where an ad essentially is in a funnel, okay?

83
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,440
So, remember that ad that was at a 2.97x, this ad right here?

84
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:12,320
Well, look at its frequency. It's

85
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,340
3.2, okay?

86
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:24,420
And keep in mind again volume, and keep in mind scale, this ad is getting essentially one third of the total spend that our top spending ad is getting, okay? So one third,

87
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:35,840
and it almost has two times the frequency, which means that people are being essentially retargeted with this ad, which would make sense why the 2.97 ROAS is so high.

88
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:51,320
And so a lot of the times, people will also kill their top spending ads, and then all of a sudden, after maybe not one day, maybe not two days, maybe not three, but a week later, their entire ad account, and the overall ROAS, which is what matters more than anything, is going down,

89
00:09:51,540 --> 00:10:01,220
and they start panicking, and they start freaking out, and it's because the ad that is doing retargeting, they don't realize is actually doing retargeting because they don't understand how to look at frequency, and as a result of that,

90
00:10:01,220 --> 00:10:06,820
the ad that was doing all the prospecting, that was getting all of the eyeballs, that was getting people into the funnel, is no longer there.

91
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:15,340
And so now you have a retargeting ad, but you don't have anything that's actually getting people into the funnel for it to retarget, and so what ends up happening? Well, performance starts to go down.

92
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:26,580
And as a result of that, you now have to start prospecting, or Meta basically says like, "Hey dude, what the hell? Like, why did you turn off that ad? Like, that was my ad to go and get cold eyeballs so I could get you a high volume of purchases."

93
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:32,440
And then when you tell Meta like, "Hey, no, don't spend on that," then it's like, "Okay, well, now I've gotta find a new ad to prospect with."

94
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:46,000
And so what can end up happening is your account can start to become unstable because Meta is now having to spend on all these different ads to figure out, "Hey, what is gonna be my new prospecting ad? Like, I just lost the ad that I was prospecting with, but now I no longer have it. So now I've gotta find a new one.", okay?

95
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:55,840
And another thing that's super important to look at as well is cost per click. A lot of the times, we will see Meta favor top-of-funnel ads that get a very good cost per click,

96
00:10:55,980 --> 00:11:02,540
and then on the inverse, you're going to see your high-ROAS, high-conversion ads usually have a higher cost per click, right?

97
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:12,140
This retargeting ad has a 2.97 ROAS, but a $2.64 cost per click. Meanwhile, this ad up here that's doing slightly more prospecting has a $1.60 cost per click.

98
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:22,480
If you take a look at this ad right here that is clearly also doing some prospecting, 1.75x, right, $1.18 cost per click, you can see Meta is definitely doing some prospecting with this ad as well.

99
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:31,720
And so what you need to understand is what is happening in the account. If you come into your account, and you filter by purchase ROAS, and this is how you decide what your good ads are,

100
00:11:31,860 --> 00:11:42,220
I mean, look at this. Some of you might be looking at like, "Oh my gosh, this ad has you know 110x ROAS," or "This ad has a 59x ROAS, like, I need to force budget on this. I need to pull this out and put this--" It's not at scale.

101
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:48,960
It's got one purchase, okay? And so it's just not significant, okay? This ad essentially got lucky, right?

102
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:59,940
We spent a dollar on this, because again, we test through CBO, which I do have an entire video on my channel, I'll have my editors pop it up, and it tells you exactly how we run our Facebook Ads using our one-campaign structure, so you can go and check that out after this video.

103
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:08,920
But just to help you understand and to put this in some kind of a digestible format, cuz I know we're ping-ponging all over the place, spend is the only indicator that we ever filter by, okay?

104
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:23,100
Spend is the number-one metric that matters most, okay? For me, and if you want to be like the actual guys that are scaling and above, or the actual brands that we scale spending millions of dollars a month on ads, we have brands spending over a million dollars a month just on one ad account, okay?

105
00:12:23,220 --> 00:12:31,380
And so, in this instance here, as you can see, we are filtering by spend to see, "Hey, what ads are the most scalable?" That is step number one.

106
00:12:31,380 --> 00:12:37,580
Step number two is to then look at your KPI metrics, which is going to be your cost per result or your purchase return on ad spend, okay?

107
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:53,260
And then from there, you need to understand the difference between them. You need to understand, okay, well this ad is at two times the scale as this one, and although the ROAS is slightly worse than this one, this ad makes sense to keep it on because it is prospecting and it is carrying a lot of the account.

108
00:12:53,460 --> 00:13:02,860
And then again, by taking a look at the secondary metrics, so things like frequency, CPM, cost per click, you're going to start to get an understanding of where these ads kind of fit in the funnel.

109
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:07,400
But I also want you to understand that a lot of times your best ads also do retargeting.

110
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:21,020
For those of you that remember, I don't even know if I made videos about this back then, but we used to have dedicated retargeting campaigns, and usually in those dedicated retargeting campaigns, ads that were doing the best in our prospecting campaigns were also doing the best in retargeting, right?

111
00:13:21,380 --> 00:13:33,580
Because they're just great ads. And so, a lot of the times, you don't-- A lot of people think you need to have specific retargeting ads, and are some ads more retargeting than others? 100%. But the point is is Meta's gonna know better than you, which is why we run ads the way that we do.

112
00:13:33,580 --> 00:13:39,820
And so hopefully this helps you understand what is going on with spend. Let's get out of this ad account, and let's go take a look at another one.

113
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:48,320
All right, cool. So as you can see, we're on screen here, and I'm now actually in the ad set level to show you how we actually test creatives. Again, we test everything through batches of ads,

114
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:57,520
but I want to show you an example here of an ad that again is absolutely murdering, okay? So the account, in this campaign specifically, is spending about 9.2k per day, okay?

115
00:13:57,740 --> 00:14:04,480
And as you can see, this ad was getting $6,800 a day in spend at a 2.03x ROAS.

116
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:15,820
Now, this is where some of you would be like, "Well, look at this, like, this ad's getting a 2.8, this ad's getting a 3.8. Look at this one: 17x ROAS, why are we not scaling that?" This is where you guys need to understand that again, Meta is very smart, okay?

117
00:14:16,020 --> 00:14:26,640
And so there are going to be instances where yes, you actually do have other ads that start getting really good performance, and I want you to keep an eye on this row specifically to see what actually happens.

118
00:14:26,860 --> 00:14:30,840
So this is August 31st. Let's go ahead and move over to September 1st.

119
00:14:30,940 --> 00:14:38,560
And, as you can see, oh, wow. Meta actually spent an extra $500, or $476 on this ad set specifically, okay?

120
00:14:38,740 --> 00:14:43,800
And, as you can see, our top-spending ad lost a bit of spend. It went from $6800 in spend to 5400,

121
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:51,900
but, as you can see, Meta started pushing more spend towards this one here. And take a look, the ROAS was a 4X compared to a 2.6.

122
00:14:52,160 --> 00:15:02,260
If we scroll to the right, you can see the frequency is relatively low, right? So, they're both essentially prospecting. And if we take a look at the cost per click, the cost per click is actually better on this one as well. Okay, well, that's interesting.

123
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:09,760
Let's see what happens if we go to the next day. So, we go to the next day, and, oh, look at this. Now, this ad is getting even more spend.

124
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:19,020
And, on top of that, we did have another ad start picking up spend as well, which I'm going to talk about a second. But focusing on the good here, you can see this ad now got 2.4k in spend,

125
00:15:19,240 --> 00:15:28,760
which is another $1,000 more than the previous day and had a better ROAS, with again, a low frequency, a better CPM, and a really, really good cost per click, okay?

126
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:37,560
And so, in this instance here, Meta is starting to see that this is a great ad. And so, if we go to the third of the month, look at this. Meta just changed our super-winner.

127
00:15:37,740 --> 00:15:43,960
And so now we have a new ad starting to get majority of spend. And, as you can see, now it's gotten $3,000 a day in spend.

128
00:15:43,960 --> 00:15:50,320
And if you take a look at this, we actually had another ad that Our team was on fire this week, so we got another ad that started picking up spend.

129
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:57,240
But, as you can see here, now this ad is getting the majority of the spend in the ad account. Meta pulled spend away from this one, which was the previous top performer.

130
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:04,600
And, as you can see, overall now, Meta, based off of frequency, is actually doing even more prospecting with this. Okay, well, let's take a look at yesterday.

131
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:13,800
Well, now if we go to yesterday, you can see that Meta is now about to do the same thing with another ad. You can see this ad up here is still the top performer. Performance is extremely strong.

132
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:21,420
And then, as you can see here, Meta has another ad that it's starting to spend on. And that's what Meta does. Meta has to find statistical significance,

133
00:16:21,420 --> 00:16:27,240
and has to find ads that, again, can get you the highest volume of purchases possible for the lowest cost.

134
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:34,580
And Meta can't do that unless it actually spends money on ads to figure out whether or not they're actually going to be good.

135
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:42,800
And just to be clear, what I mean by that is that Meta actually doesn't need to spend on your ads at all if it knows that they're not good, okay?

136
00:16:42,980 --> 00:16:50,980
Meta is not even going to waste its time or, better yet, it's not going to waste your money on ads that it just knows it has no shot of scaling, okay?

137
00:16:51,020 --> 00:17:02,240
So if you've also ever seen ads get like $0 in spend, kind of like these ads down here that are getting $0.12 in spend or $1.60 in spend or $2.95 in spend.

138
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:11,420
Like for example, this ad has been live since August 28th, and it's gotten essentially no spend at all. Now, in those instances, Meta is just telling you like, "Hey, dude, sorry. Try again.

139
00:17:11,580 --> 00:17:20,320
Like, I'm not going to waste your money." And this is the craziest part to me, is so many of you guys are like, "I have to spend money on these." And it's like, there's no point. Like Meta is trying to save you money.

140
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:28,200
But what I meant with my point is that Meta is going to have to spend on ads that it's like, "Hey, you know what? This might be a good ad. Like, hold on a second.

141
00:17:28,340 --> 00:17:35,880
This thing is getting an $0.80 cost per click. It seems like a lot of people are engaging with it, and this has an opportunity based off of my billions of data points

142
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:44,240
